# NON-FUNCTIONAL STRAWMAN (imagination-tier; see README.md). The declined case:
# an author's deliberate refusal, rendered as a first-class ANSWER rather than
# an absence. The design burden: the pull surface must be RICHER than the plan's
# one-line push (today it is poorer — the gallery's fnd-decline-class-is-push-only).
# The author's voice arrives as their own code and comments (show-the-code);
# the engine adds the anti-nag statement and the model-offer, and stops.

-- book.sh (context, abbreviated) --
 6  sysctl -w vm.drop_caches=3

-- oracle (context) --
sysctl.oracle.sh declines the drop_caches key as [unsound], with a comment and
a report-lane breadcrumb on the arm.

-- replay --
$ dorc why 6
book.sh:6  sysctl -w vm.drop_caches=3
  runs on every apply — permanently, and by judgment rather than by gap.

  1. declined   sysctl's oracle deliberately refuses to answer for this
                shape  [unsound: permanently unanswerable]
                ┌ sysctl.oracle.sh:23 — the author's own arm, as written:
                │   vm.drop_caches|vm.compact_memory)
                │      # write-only trigger: writing is an action, not state.
                │      # the kernel doesn't retain it; "is it already done"
                │      # has no answer, ever.
                │      printf 'decline unsound %s is a write-only trigger key\n' "$key" \
                │         >>"${DREP_V1:-/dev/null}"
                │      return 2 ;;
                └ shown as authored; comments are the author's words, not dorc's
  2. derived    a cannot-say always runs: nothing may be removed from a plan
                without an authored yes.

  no oracle improvement changes this line, and dorc will not suggest one —
  the person who knows this tool ruled the question unanswerable.

  if you expected convergence here: sysctl's model answers for RETAINED
  state as two cells — @value (the running kernel) and @persisted (the boot
  config) — and this key has neither. a retained key probes and elides:
      sysctl -w vm.swappiness=10        # probeable; elides when converged
  see:  dorc explain declines · dorc explain write-only-triggers
